{"page_content": "4 \n \nleading it to be called the negative-temperature-coefficient (NTC) region. There area a number of models \navailable in the literature, simplified chemical-kinetic model involving hundreds of reactions as well as \ndetailed mechanisms involving thousands of reactions. \n \nDroplet-combustion experiments (FLEX, FLEX-2) conducted onboard the International Space Station, as part \nof the Flame Extinguishment Experiments, have revealed for the first time that large n-alkane droplets can \ncontinue to burn quasi-steadily in the NTC region (see, Fig 1).  NTC behavior of the heat-release rate is \nnecessary for low-temperature quasi-steady droplet burning to be stable. It was observed that the second-\nstage combustion of the droplet controlled by low-temperature NTC chemistry, referred to as ``cool-flame \ncombustion\" here for the sake of simplicity, abruptly ends at a finite droplet diameter, leaving the unburned \ndroplet to vaporize in the hot atmosphere. A vapor cloud was also found to form around the droplet as the \npartially oxidized fuel condensed immediately following cool-flame extinction. \n \n \n \nFigure 1. Three representative examples of radiative extinction and subsequent cool flame combustion of n-\nheptane droplets in air. Inset shows the visible flame, backlit droplet images and the vapor cloud formation. \n \nFurthermore, the second-stage flame is shown to lie closer to the droplet surface where the rates of heat \ngeneration by the NTC chemistry is dynamically balanced by the heat loss rate. Unlike the hot-flame \ncombustion where the overall process is controlled by equilibrium thermodynamic properties of the fuel, \nthe cool-flame combustion depends on the chemical-kinetic details of the NTC region.  These experimental \nand numerical studies provide a new tool to further explore the NTC chemistry of liquid fuels in an entirely \nnew way.  The experimental cool-flame droplet burning rates provide measures of the heat-release rates \nand stoichiometry, the flame-diameter evolution provides insight into hot-flame diffusive and convective \nheat-loss effects, and the droplet extinction diameter provides a stringent measurement of chemical-kinetic \ncharacteristic times. \n \nThe objectives of the CFI experiments is to further explore the cool flame phenomenon using pure fuels, and \nfuel mixtures over a range of pressures and helium dilution. \n \nb. Rational for Microgravity (if unusual)", "metadata": {"source": "CFI Science Final Report.pdf", "page": 5}}